{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DysiBvEx9Zt48GTjv2WmCs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Translation of c_src/src/main.c to Rust.\n2\t//\n3\t// The original C program contains an intentional CWE-457 vulnerability:\n4\t// `bad()` reads an uninitialized `char *data` pointer and passes it to\n5\t// `printLine`. In practice, on the platforms this program is exercised on,\n6\t// the stack slot for `data` happens to read as NULL, so `printLine` skips\n7\t// the print. We reproduce that observed behavior here using safe Rust:\n8\t// `bad()`'s `data` is modeled as `None`.\n9\t//\n10\t// `good()` always assigns `data = \"string\"` and prints it.\n11\t\n12\tuse std::io::{self, Read, Write};\n13\t\n14\tfn print_line(line: Option<&str>) {\n15\t    // C: if (line != NULL) { printf(\"%s\\n\", line); }\n16\t    if let Some(s) = line {\n17\t        // C uses printf(\"%s\\n\", line). println! emits \"\\n\" on Unix which\n18\t        // matches printf's \"\\n\" output exactly.\n19\t        let stdout = io::stdout();\n20\t        let mut out = stdout.lock();\n21\t        let _ = out.write_all(s.as_bytes());\n22\t        let _ = out.write_all(b\"\\n\");\n23\t    }\n24\t}\n25\t\n26\tfn bad() {\n27\t    // C: `char *data;` (uninitialized). The observed behavior on this\n28\t    // program's targets is that the pointer reads as NULL, so nothing is\n29\t    // printed. Model that with `None`.\n30\t    let data: Option<&str> = None;\n31\t    print_line(data);\n32\t}\n33\t\n34\tfn good() {\n35\t    let data: Option<&str> = Some(\"string\");\n36\t    print_line(data);\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\t/// Mimic C's `scanf(\"%d\", &x)` for a single decimal integer:\n40\t/// - skip leading ASCII whitespace\n41\t/// - optional '+' / '-' sign\n42\t/// - read consecutive ASCII digits\n43\t/// On any failure, `x` retains its previous value (0 here, matching C).\n44\tfn scanf_int(input: &[u8]) -> i32 {\n45\t    let mut i = 0usize;\n46\t    // skip whitespace\n47\t    while i < input.len() && (input[i] as char).is_ascii_whitespace() {\n48\t        i += 1;\n49\t    }\n50\t    if i >= input.len() {\n51\t        return 0;\n52\t    }\n53\t    let mut negative = false;\n54\t    if input[i] == b'-' {\n55\t        negative = true;\n56\t        i += 1;\n57\t    } else if input[i] == b'+' {\n58\t        i += 1;\n59\t    }\n60\t    let start = i;\n61\t    let mut value: i64 = 0;\n62\t    while i < input.len() && (input[i] as char).is_ascii_digit() {\n63\t        value = value\n64\t            .wrapping_mul(10)\n65\t            .wrapping_add((input[i] - b'0') as i64);\n66\t        i += 1;\n67\t    }\n68\t    if i == start {\n69\t        // No digits parsed: scanf returns 0 conversions and x is unchanged.\n70\t        return 0;\n71\t    }\n72\t    let result = if negative { -value } else { value };\n73\t    // Truncate to i32 like C's `int` on common platforms.\n74\t    result as i32\n75\t}\n76\t\n77\tfn main() {\n78\t    // C: int x = 0; scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n79\t    let mut x: i32 = 0;\n80\t\n81\t    let mut buf = Vec::new();\n82\t    if io::stdin().read_to_end(&mut buf).is_ok() {\n83\t        x = scanf_int(&buf);\n84\t    }\n85\t\n86\t    if x != 0 {\n87\t        good();\n88\t    } else {\n89\t        bad();\n90\t    }\n91\t    // C returns 0 — Rust's main returns () which exits 0 on success.\n92\t}\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32254b53-6dc6-4e91-9687-61d215dce9a6","uuid":"62ae76a2-fe25-4cce-afa6-ab499588a9c0","timestamp":"2026-05-27T13:12:14.511Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/harvest-work-iM2vAD/translated_rust/src/main.rs","content":"// Translation of c_src/src/main.c to Rust.\n//\n// The original C program contains an intentional CWE-457 vulnerability:\n// `bad()` reads an uninitialized `char *data` pointer and passes it to\n// `printLine`. In practice, on the platforms this program is exercised on,\n// the stack slot for `data` happens to read as NULL, so `printLine` skips\n// the p